::Q1::What is the key difference between a Pandas Series and a DataFrame?
{
~A Series is two-dimensional while a DataFrame is one-dimensional
=A Series is a one-dimensional labeled array, while a DataFrame is a two-dimensional table of Series
~A Series can contain multiple columns while a DataFrame can contain only one
~A Series is used only for numerical data while a DataFrame is used only for text
}

::Q2::What is the difference between loc and iloc when selecting rows from a Pandas DataFrame?
{
~loc selects by integer position and iloc selects by label
=loc selects by label and iloc selects by integer position
~Both select only columns by name
~Both always select rows by integer position
}

::Q3::When filtering a DataFrame on two conditions at once, such as price greater than 10 and quantity less than 50, what is the correct way to combine the conditions?
{
~Use the Python keyword "and" to join the two conditions directly
=Wrap each condition in parentheses and join them with the "&" operator
~Use the Python keyword "and" without wrapping either condition in parentheses
~Use the "|" operator instead of "&" to join the two conditions
}

::Q4::What does calling isna() followed by sum() on a DataFrame provide?
{
~The total number of rows in the DataFrame
=The number of missing values in each column
~A DataFrame containing only rows with missing values
~The average value of each column
}

::Q5::Which Pandas operation most directly corresponds to SQL's GROUP BY?
{
~merge()
~loc[]
=groupby()
~dropna()
}

::Q6::When merging two DataFrames called orders and customers on the customer_id column using a left join, with orders as the left DataFrame, what is the result?
{
~Only rows with matching customer IDs are retained
=Every row from orders is retained, with matching customer information added when available
~Every row from customers is retained and unmatched orders are removed
~Both DataFrames are combined without using customer_id
}